def _get_declared_instance_attributes(subject: type) -> set[str]:
    """
    Return the names of attributes instances of *subject* are known to have, even if
    those attributes are not present on the class object itself.

    The following are considered:

    * class level (or inherited) annotations, e.g. ``foo: int``
    * attributes declared in ``__slots__``
    * attributes assigned to objects in any function defined in the class (or any of
      its base classes), most typically assignments to ``self`` in ``__init__``

    This is needed when a class is checked against a protocol (i.e. against
    ``type[SomeProtocol]``) as protocol members that are only assigned to the instance
    cannot be retrieved from the class object.
